---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-vaccine-response`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Vaccine Response',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Vaccine Response', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Vaccine Response: Does Correcting Deficiency Boost Antibody Titres?"
  description="What the evidence shows about vitamin D status and vaccine antibody response — flu, COVID, hepatitis B, and whether repletion before vaccination is worth it."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-vaccine-response", label: "Vitamin D and Vaccine Response" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Vaccine Response</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Vaccine effectiveness varies from person to person. Deficiencies in
      micronutrients that support immune function — vitamin D among them — have
      been examined as potentially reducing vaccine antibody response. Trial
      evidence for vitamin D supplementation before vaccination is limited but
      modestly favourable in deficient adults.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The biology</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Vitamin D receptors are expressed on activated T-cells and B-cells. Calcitriol
      modulates the balance between Treg, Th1, Th2, and Th17 responses. Adequate
      vitamin D status supports both innate (cathelicidin) and adaptive (antibody
      production, T-cell function) immunity. Deficiency doesn't abolish vaccine
      response but may modestly reduce it in a subset of patients.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Flu vaccine evidence</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Several observational studies find modestly lower post-vaccination antibody
      titres in vitamin D-deficient adults. The Chadha 2021 trial randomised older
      adults to vitamin D or placebo before influenza vaccination; the vitamin D
      group had modestly higher post-vaccination titres to some strains. The
      overall clinical significance is small.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">COVID vaccine</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Multiple observational studies examined vitamin D and mRNA COVID vaccine
      response. The most consistent finding: vitamin D-deficient adults had
      slightly lower peak antibody titres and slightly faster decline. Whether
      this reduced real-world protection is unclear — antibody titre correlates
      imperfectly with vaccine effectiveness.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Hepatitis B vaccine</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Well-studied vaccine with clear correlates of protection (anti-HBs
      titre &gt; 10 mIU/mL). Vitamin D deficiency associated with hepatitis B
      vaccine non-response in dialysis patients (Zitt 2012). Repletion before
      revaccination improved seroconversion in retrospective series.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical guidance</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Correct vitamin D deficiency for many reasons, one of which is optimal immune function.</li>
      <li>Do not delay a vaccine to correct vitamin D deficiency first — the delay is riskier than the modest immunogenicity effect.</li>
      <li>Chronic vaccine non-responders (hepatitis B, e.g., dialysis patients): checking and correcting vitamin D is a reasonable step alongside standard revaccination.</li>
      <li>Do not use vitamin D as a rationale for avoiding vaccines — it complements them, doesn't replace them.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Vaccine decisions are best made with your
      physician; nutritional adequacy supports vaccine response but does not
      substitute for immunisation.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-vaccine-response" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
